Kernir patch 0021: the iso9660 reader reads a directory one block at a time and holds its records, Rockridge names and link targets to Kernir's limits

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_iso9660.c (read_children, choose_volume, read_CE,
struct file_info, struct iso9660); include/kernir_names.h (a comment). Applies on top of 0001-0020.

The reader reads a whole directory, and every Rockridge continuation area (CE) its records point at, before it returns
the directory's first entry, so no limit of the caller's comes in time:
- read_children read ahead the directory's whole extent at once: its size is a 32-bit on-disk value checked only
  against the volume size the image declares itself, so a 296 KiB image claiming a 4 GiB directory had the
  read-ahead copy buffer malloc 4.3 GB before EOF (any image past libarchive's first read, 64 KiB for Kernir);
  choose_volume did the same with the root's size. The extent is now read and
  parsed one logical block at a time (current_position still moves to the extent's end first, so register_CE takes
  the same CE areas as before), so a short file stops at EOF with bounded memory, and choose_volume reads only the
  root's directory record (at most 255 bytes).
- every record of the directory was queued with no count: each one, the root included, now counts against 0016's
  entry limit before parse_file_info allocates it, refused with "ISO9660 image has more entries than the entry
  limit". Multi-extent parts, CL placeholders and a hidden rr_moved count too, though no header of theirs is
  returned, so such an image can be refused slightly before Kernir's own count would refuse it.
- several records' CE entries can point at one chain of CE blocks, and read_CE then appends each block's NM and SL
  text to every one of those names: N records and k blocks made N x k x 1.75 KB of names from k x 2 KB of file (an 848
  KB image built 583 MB of names). Each record's name and link target are now charged after parse_file_info and after
  each continuation area, by the most they have held (an NM without CONTINUE empties a name, and the charge is not
  given back): a name or link target over 65,536 bytes is refused with "ISO9660 entry name is longer than 65536
  bytes" / "ISO9660 link target is longer than 65536 bytes", and all of them over 0016's names total
  (__archive_read_kernir_names_allowance of the records so far) with "ISO9660 entry names and link targets take more
  than the names limit".
- a shared chain whose areas grow no name (padding, unknown entries, or NM without CONTINUE refilling one name) still
  had read_CE parse N x k areas, CPU quadratic in the image's size before the first header, with nothing charged:
  each record's pass over each area is now charged by the area's size before it is parsed, to a total of its own held
  to the same allowance plus the bytes read so far (current_position: a valid image's distinct areas, such as
  xorriso's multi-KB AAIP ACL and xattr entries, always fit in those, and a shared chain stays linear in the image's
  size) and refused with the same message.
All refusals are ARCHIVE_FATAL, ARCHIVE_ERRNO_FILE_FORMAT; Kernir maps the messages to its entry-count, path-length
and names-total limits. Images within the limits read as before.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0021-iso9660-directory-limits.patch

